<h2>Part 3: List of Visual Encodings, Units, and Level of Measurement</h2>

<h3>1. Time (Quarteryear)</h3>
<p><strong>Unit:</strong> Yearquarter</p>
<p><strong>Level of measurement:</strong> Interval</p>
<p>Quarters can be sorted, and the intervals of adjacent quarters are the same, but there is no zero point when time completely disappears, so it cannot be said that a certain point of time is several times that of another point of time. It is only suitable for calculating how long the difference is, not for multiples.</p>

<h3>2. Debt category</h3>
<p><strong>Unit:</strong> No unit</p>
<p><strong>Level of measurement:</strong> Nominal</p>
<p>Categorical labels for six debt types (Mortgage, HE Revolving, Auto Loan, Credit Card, Student Loan, Other) with no inherent ranking or quantitative difference.</p>

<h3>3. Percentage change in household debt (relative to Q12003 baseline)</h3>
<p><strong>Unit:</strong> Percentage (%)</p>
<p><strong>Level of measurement:</strong> Ratio</p>
<p>The percentage change has a meaningful zero point (0% means no change compared to the benchmark value in the first quarter of 2003), and ratios are interpretable (e.g., a 100% increase is twice the magnitude of a 50% increase).</p>


<h2>Part 4: Data and Question Alignment</h2>
<p>Following the Junk Charts Trifecta Checkup framework (Fung, 2014), the alignment between the Data (D) and the Question (Q) in this visualisation is strong.</p>

<p><strong>The Question(Q):</strong> HowMuch.net asks: "How has total household debt changed across the U.S. over the last several years?" This visualization further breaks down this broad issue to analyze the percentage change trends for each debt category from the first quarter of 2003 to the fourth quarter of 2020.</p>

<p><strong>The Data(D):</strong> The visualisation is based on data in the Household Debt and Credit Report released by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York. The original source provides debt balances denominated in U.S. dollars, and HowMuch.net calculates percentage‑change data for various debt categories (including student loans, auto loans and mortgage loans) covering 2003 to 2020. The dataset is directly relevant to the question.</p>

<p>The data is highly relevant to the question, because the variables (debt category, percentage change and time) directly correspond to the information needed to answer how household debt changes. The time range is clearly defined (20032020), and a common reference quarter (Q12003) is adopted to facilitate the comparison across different categories. However, one inherent limitation of the underlying dataset is that the visualization presents a summary trend at the national level and does not subdivide the changes according to population or geographic subgroups, which may limit a more detailed understanding of the data.</p>


